[Section: 13. CMake — first strong direct evidence > Weaknesses]

- old global-style CMake;

- legacy minimum version;

- suspicious `set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS, ...)` syntax;

- no target-scoped modern CMake;

- no test target.

[Section: 34. Source tree / architecture — expanded > Cross-track limitation]

There is no software layer connecting the radar notebook to the LiDAR C++ pipeline. The repository contains **two sensor-learning tracks**, not a fused runtime architecture.

[Section: 42. Product / perception failure potential]

If used only as an educational simulator, consequences are limited. In a real autonomous system, analogous weaknesses could become safety-critical:

- ROI excludes a relevant obstacle;

- RANSAC threshold misclassifies road/obstacle points;

- clustering merges/splits objects;

- visualization-loop defect masks runtime health;

- no automated regression detects parameter regressions;

- no hardware calibration/synchronization evidence;

- no fusion or uncertainty model.

This is why the corpus must distinguish **perception algorithm practice** from safety-validated autonomous-driving software.

[Section: 45. Product / engineering failure-mode register]

| Failure mode | Likely effect | Evidence status |

| Viewer event loop misplaced | frozen/unresponsive visualization | directly observed in final structure |

| ROI too restrictive | missed obstacles | parameter-risk inference |

| Clustering tolerance too high | merged objects | algorithm/configuration risk |

| Clustering tolerance too low | fragmented objects | algorithm/configuration risk |

| No regression tests | silent parameter regressions | directly absent |

| Raw allocation | leaks/lifetime ambiguity | directly observed pattern |

| Old CMake assumptions | build incompatibility | observed style/risk |

